00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.950
The United States Navy is traditionally known
for its military might.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:19.810
It is, by far, the largest and potentially
most capable Navy in the world thanks to its

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:23.130
innumerable aircraft carriers, destroyers,
and submarines.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:29.221
But, beyond those, and beyond all the cruisers
and patrol boats and other combat ships, there

00:00:29.221 --> 00:00:37.140
are two more that lurk in the shadows of the
490 ship fleet—the USNS Mercy and Comfort.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.140
These are America’s hospital ships.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.530
Hospital ships are more or less a forgotten
relic of a time when wars were far deadlier

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.690
and the world was far larger.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:51.230
During those times, when fighting a war abroad,
just like today, one had to consider where

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:56.199
casualties would go, but unlike today, there
were no capabilities to quickly fly injured

00:00:56.199 --> 00:00:58.410
personnel away from a battlefield.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:03.019
One had to treat close to the conflict and
for that, the most viable option was often

00:01:03.019 --> 00:01:06.141
to bring a hospital-ship to the fight.

00:01:06.141 --> 00:01:09.830
These ships were once so significant that,
when nations came together to write the Geneva

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:14.710
Conventions, defining the rules of war, hospital
ships were a significant focus.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:19.080
They specifically denote these as any that
specifically and solely work to assist the

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.410
wounded or sick, with the important distinction
that they cannot carry any arms or ammunition.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:29.080
These ships also must be painted white with
red crosses to identify themselves, but assuming

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:33.280
these requirements are met, they are officially
classed as a hospital ship.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.990
Those with this classification cannot, under
the Geneva Conventions, be attacked or fired

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:39.110
upon.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:43.820
This was all plenty useful at the time the
Geneva Conventions were signed, but today,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:47.909
land-based warfare is increasingly obsolete,
and it just doesn’t make sense to float

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.990
a hospital to a conflict zone when field hospitals
are more capable, and medical evacuation is

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:53.990
easy.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:58.250
That’s why only eight nations’ navies
still include hospital ships.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:02.750
Most of these are rather small and primarily
deployed for humanitarian purposes, but the

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.750
US’ two hospital ships, meanwhile, are massive.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:12.180
They’re the size of cruise ships and comparable
in capability to the largest land-based hospitals.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.599
Each holds 1,000 hospital beds including 80
intensive care beds.

00:02:16.599 --> 00:02:23.849
In comparison, the entire state of Maine has
1,061 total and 64 ICU beds.

00:02:23.849 --> 00:02:28.489
To have an entire state’s worth of medical
facilities available anywhere in the world

00:02:28.489 --> 00:02:33.709
is an immensely useful strategic power, but
not entirely for the purpose of actually treating

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:35.130
US soldiers.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:40.090
Within the military, the Comfort and Mercy
are officially classified as, “flexible

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:41.379
deterrent options.”

00:02:41.379 --> 00:02:44.459
That's to say, they’re an option for, “show
of force.”

00:02:44.459 --> 00:02:49.430
In wartime, sending a ship with capacity for
a thousand patients sends a message that the

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:53.420
US is willing to accept an immense amount
of casualties.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.650
That may or may not be true, but the message
is there.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:01.569
In peacetime, parading a hospital ship around
the world for humanitarian missions both fosters

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:06.579
good will for the US military, and also shows
countries the capabilities of the US’ military

00:03:06.579 --> 00:03:07.579
health system.

00:03:07.579 --> 00:03:12.099
The effectiveness of these ships as “flexible
deterrent options” could be debatable, but

00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:15.900
in the military’s mind, this is the purpose
behind these ships.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.260
They’re an option for deterrence or escalation.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:24.200
That’s part of the reason why they’ve
seen so little deployment to combat zones.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.060
That’s just not the primary purpose.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.459
Each ship has only seen one deployment primarily
treating casualties of war—the Mercy to

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:37.230
Operation Desert Shield and Storm in 1990,
and the Comfort to the Iraq War in 2002.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:41.569
For the rest of their 30 year histories, they
have spent their time deployed on humanitarian

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:46.299
missions to disasters or impoverished nations,
or in reduced operating status at their home

00:03:46.299 --> 00:03:48.849
ports in San Diego and Norfolk.

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:53.629
While during active service the Mercy houses
well over 1,000 crew, most of its time is

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:57.239
spent in port, in that reduced operating status.

00:03:57.239 --> 00:04:01.559
At such times, the massive ships will house
just 70 crew members who will keep it ready

00:04:01.559 --> 00:04:03.769
to go at a moment’s notice.

00:04:03.769 --> 00:04:07.540
By the nature of their service, expediency
of deployment is key.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:11.989
Therefore, they’re designed so that they
can go from reduced operating status to leaving

00:04:11.989 --> 00:04:15.489
San Diego or Norfolk in just five days.

00:04:15.489 --> 00:04:20.350
But while they can leave quickly, that doesn’t
mean they can necessarily get to their destination

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:21.350
quickly.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:26.830
The Mercy and Comfort are both converted oil
tankers that originally launched in 1975 and

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:28.290
1976, respectively.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:32.670
Oil tankers are not designed for speed, as
it’s just not necessary, so the ships can

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:37.010
only travel at about 20 miles or 31 kilometers
per hour.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:41.910
Compare that to a Nimitz-class aircraft carrier
which, at almost twice the size, can travel

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:45.340
at 35 miles or 56 kilometers per hour.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:49.970
If there was, say, a disaster in Sri Lanka,
it would take five days to get the ship into

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:55.060
full operating status, and then 20 to sail
all the way to the Indian Ocean.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:05:00.090
By that time, nearly a month after the disaster,
the trauma phase of medical response would

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:01.510
be largely done.

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:05.540
This is likely a reason why a majority of
the ships’ deployments have been near the

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:07.620
US—in the Americas.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:12.430
Of those, many have been long-planned humanitarian
missions to provide advanced healthcare to

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.120
areas without it.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:18.670
These are, of course, incredibly impactful
for those they serve, but overall, the ships

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:22.930
just aren’t designed for super-fast response
to critical crises.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:27.320
The very size that slows it does, however,
have a purpose.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:32.210
Beyond just allowing for a massive capacity,
the ships are stable enough that, even when

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:37.200
floating, it is possible to conduct most types
of surgery in their 12 operating rooms.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.590
There are exceptions—the floating platform
doesn’t allow for open-heart surgery—but

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:47.850
still, the possibility of conducting advanced
medical interventions at sea is impressive.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:51.670
There are, however, more issues related to
the origins of the ships.

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:56.140
You see, hospitals are quite tricky facilities
to design.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:59.780
Small and subtle differences can mean the
difference between life and death, and so

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:04.140
the accumulated experience of hundreds of
years has resulted in explicit knowledge on

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.670
how hospitals should be built.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:11.280
One of the myriad principles is that movement
bottlenecks should be minimized.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.460
That leads to features like wide hallways
and doors, but it also means that, as much

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.770
as possible, the use of elevators should be
avoided.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:22.850
The technique to achieve that is, of course,
to make hospitals wider.

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:27.530
You want as much as possible on the same floor
so that there is as little need as possible

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:31.930
to put patients in elevators when transferring
them from one area to the other.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:36.430
If, for example, a patient takes a turn for
the worse and needs to be rushed to an operating

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:40.690
room, the 90 seconds waiting for and riding
the elevator can mean the difference between

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:42.240
life and death.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.150
This also has an implication on overall hospital
efficiency.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:51.310
For example, you ideally want radiology rather
close to and on the same floor as the emergency

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:56.400
room since many patients often need x-ray
imaging right when they present to the ER.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.050
The further away it is, the longer it takes
to get a patient to and from there, and then

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:03.960
the longer they spend in the ER department
overall, and the more beds are needed to serve

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.620
the same number of patients in the ER.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:10.830
These principles are all why hospitals tend
to be wide.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:15.061
Even in places with incredible density and
high real-estate costs like New York, most

00:07:15.061 --> 00:07:17.810
hospitals are wider than they are tall.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:22.350
In all, hospital design is a puzzle where
all the elements that patients most commonly

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:26.970
are transferred between need to be as close,
in travel time, to each other.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:31.840
Looking at the outside of the USNS Mercy and
Comfort, you would think that they would properly

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.720
follow this principle of width before height,
but unfortunately, during their conversion,

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.480
legacies of their past lives were left behind.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:45.139
You see, tanker ships, on the inside, are
not just one big empty hull filled with oil.

00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:49.690
They were when they first emerged, many years
ago, but the issue there was that, with a

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:55.420
slight tilt forwards, backwards, or to a side,
oil would start sloshing to that side, which

00:07:55.420 --> 00:08:00.230
leads to more weight on that side, which leads
to more tilt, which leads to more oil sloshing,

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:03.680
and so on and so forth until a ship capsized.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:07.960
That was clearly an issue, but one easily
solved by creating bulkheads both laterally

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:11.580
and longitudinally so that oil couldn’t
slosh around.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:15.700
The problem, though, is the these were left
in the Comfort and Mercy.

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:19.160
There are no doors between bulkheads on the
lower floors.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:23.950
Therefore, if one needs to get a patient from
here to here, they have to be brought up an

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:27.420
elevator, across the top deck, and back down
again.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:32.229
This leads to huge movement issues, making
the ship’s effectiveness as a hospital more

00:08:32.229 --> 00:08:33.229
limited.

00:08:33.229 --> 00:08:37.680
That’s just one large example, but there
are plenty of other smaller issues with the

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.470
ship designs that make them less effective
than they could be.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:45.220
They are, after all, nearly 50 years old,
and the Navy was already starting to plan

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.490
their retirement decades ago.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:53.120
Their original cost, including both the ship
purchase and conversion, was $275 million

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:58.750
each, which may seem like a lot, but this
is the same Navy that spends billions on single

00:08:58.750 --> 00:08:59.750
destroyers.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:04.690
A couple hundred million is a bargain for
a ship of this size and, yearly, its operating

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:07.890
costs are rather insignificant in the context
of the Navy.

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:12.490
It’s the kind of asset that makes sense
to keep around just in case it becomes useful,

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:16.800
as it does every once in a while when disaster
strikes in the US or abroad, but also one

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.230
that the US is likely to be unwilling to invest
in upgrading.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:25.010
A dedicated, purpose-built hospital ship of
this size could be an enormous step up from

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:29.550
these, but when given the choice between a
new destroyer or a new hospital ship, the

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.820
Navy’s going to pick the destroyer.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:35.870
It’s for that reason why the Comfort and
Mercy have stayed in service for so long,

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:39.320
and why they’re likely to stay in service
until the very last day possible.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:44.610
They may not be of enormous use in the Navy’s
eyes, but to the thousands that they treat,

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:49.130
they’re a symbol of hope in tough times
and, until they’re incapable of helping

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:55.400
anyone, they serve a crucial purpose.

